- My heart is breaking.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.723
- Residents nowadays make
somewhere in the range of,

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:10.340
I would say 40 to 65 000, depending where

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.470
your program is, what
kind of program it is.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:15.030
Now there's hour
restrictions in place, where,

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.350
interns especially can only
work 16 hour shifts at a time.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.870
You have to have a certain amount of hours

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.520
in between your shifts,
there's weekly caps.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.100
As I mentioned in my Grey's
Anatomy review video,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.926
the Libby Zion Law in New York State

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:31.770
started this trend of instituting
hourly caps on residents,

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.190
and it spread to all 50
states at this point.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.090
- [Pager] Dr Green, your wife
is waiting in the cafeteria.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:39.004
- Dammit.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.110
- We still have pagers, and sometimes

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.170
you can send them messages to your phones.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:45.450
But the reason they were trying

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:46.997
to reduce the amount of pagers overhead

00:05:46.997 --> 00:05:50.160
is to allow patients to rest and sleep.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.960
If you think about it,
who's in the hospital?

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:52.793
Sick people.

00:05:52.793 --> 00:05:54.070
People who need to get better.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.160
How do they get better?

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.730
Through rest, because body heals itself.

00:05:57.730 --> 00:05:59.980
If you constantly have
pages going on overhead,

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:01.940
it's very difficult
for a patient to sleep.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.223
- Oh now, would you look at this?

00:06:07.129 --> 00:06:10.280
That's the first tailored
white coat I've ever seen.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.210
- This is really funny.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.430
So, Noah Wyle, this actor.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:18.277
People tell me all the time
how much I remind them of him.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:20.870
- I probably get that more than I get

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.010
the whole McDreamy thing, even though

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.270
the McDreamy thing's a running joke,

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.180
but people say I look so much like him.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:26.710
This first comment that I see of them

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:27.691
is them making fun of the fact

00:06:27.691 --> 00:06:29.459
that he has a tailored white coat.

00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:31.932
And I actually have a white
coat that's very fitted,

00:06:31.932 --> 00:06:33.990
and my nurses and my colleagues

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.158
always make fun of me, like, ooh,

00:06:35.158 --> 00:06:37.761
look at Doctor Mike with
his fancy white coat.

00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:40.660
I just think it's important
to look professional, clean,

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.100
and you know, suited up as a doctor.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.640
You know, you work hard, you wanna

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:45.290
preserve this image of professionalism.

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.520
So I don't think it's a bad thing.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.660
And I know they're gonna
pick on him 'cause,

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.609
I've experienced people
picking me about it.

00:06:50.609 --> 00:06:52.070
- It's an Angiocath with a 16 needle.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:53.183
You need a large for in
case they are bleeding

00:06:53.183 --> 00:06:54.560
and you need to transfuse them.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:55.752
Do you know how to start an IV?

00:06:55.752 --> 00:06:57.520
- Actually no.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:58.470
- I thought you were third year.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.770
- I am, but all I've done is
dermatology and psychiatry.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:03.580
- As a medical student,
in your first two years

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.835
you're pretty much in a classroom

00:07:04.835 --> 00:07:06.640
and just learning the curriculum,

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.220
everything you need to
know about the human body.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.550
- In your third year, you start doing

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.070
your clinical rotations,
especially your cores.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.360
Family medicine, internal
medicine, surgery.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.377
It's not unusual for a third year

00:07:16.377 --> 00:07:18.660
to come in and not know how to do an IV.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.740
And generally, they're trained by either

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.090
senior students, or young
residents or interns.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.230
It's scary, being on your
rotation for the first time.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.850
The way that I came about
it, and I had some success,

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:31.878
was that I came very
very excited to learn.

00:07:31.878 --> 00:07:33.617
And I wanted to learn how to do IVs,

00:07:33.617 --> 00:07:35.940
and I wanted to learn
how to do procedures.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.800
And anything that
somebody would let me do,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.540
or let me watch, or let me practice,

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.230
I would be the first to volunteer.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.500
I wouldn't try and steal
opportunities from other people,

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.890
'cause I don't wanna be a gunner.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.640
And a gunner's a person who tries to,

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.680
put other people down, or get
themselves ahead at all costs.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.160
Even if it hurts someone else.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:54.200
Wasn't one of those people.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:55.820
But I was very motivated.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:56.653
And if you're a medical student,

00:07:56.653 --> 00:07:59.580
and you wanna shine on
rotations, be motivated.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.293
- Okay ma'am, we are gonna
push on the next one, okay?

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.853
One, two, three.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:12.190
- The best way to deliver a
baby is during the contractions,

00:08:12.190 --> 00:08:14.850
you should tell the patient to push,

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:16.920
and you want them to hold the pressure

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.550
for the push as much as possible.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:20.150
And then if there's time, they can exhale,

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.510
gather their strength, and push again

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.130
while the contraction is going on.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.310
Pushing while the
contraction is not going on

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.017
is not ideal because the uterus

00:08:27.017 --> 00:08:29.060
is not contracting the baby out.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:29.893
Therefore you're not gonna have

00:08:29.893 --> 00:08:31.910
any real success in getting the baby out.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:35.410
And is that a newborn baby.

00:08:35.410 --> 00:08:37.573
It's a very large baby, you could tell.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.050
So now what we do in the hospitals, is,

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.920
if the baby's crying,
and the baby's breathing,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.350
the first thing we do is right away

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.920
put the baby on the mom's breast.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.080
That has shown the best results.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.330
Bonding with the baby is very important.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.750
It calms the mother down,
it calms the baby down,

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.366
it's one of the things that we're

00:08:56.366 --> 00:08:59.363
really pushing in all the
major hospital systems.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.510
- Nice and red.

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:05.400
He has otitis media, you'll be fine.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.700
- Otitis media is a very
common ear infection

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:11.020
in young children, it's
when the middle ear

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:14.230
becomes infected with usually
a bacterial infection.

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:16.436
You do need antibiotics for it because

00:09:16.436 --> 00:09:19.980
it can actually spread to the
local tissues, to the bone.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:23.070
There's a part of the skull
called the mastoid process

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.550
and you can get mastoiditis there.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:25.774
It can be very dangerous, especially

00:09:25.774 --> 00:09:27.590
when a baby's so young and fragile,

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:29.030
especially in the early days of life.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.290
- Everyone has a family, Mark.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.785
My wife wants to shop, kids grow up.

00:09:33.785 --> 00:09:36.556
You go to private school, college.

00:09:36.556 --> 00:09:38.680
You gotta think about that.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:42.480
Now, the office for our
next associate is here.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.740
- The constant dilemma for ER physicians

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.360
is that you often work very long hours.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.290
The hours that you do
work are very stressful.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.140
You're seeing a lot of patients.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.440
There's a high stress rate in this

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:56.690
type of job because you never know

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.130
what kind of patients you're gonna get.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.579
What this doctor's
essentially offering Dr Green

00:10:00.579 --> 00:10:05.060
is to leave the ER, work
as an outpatient physician.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.370
Where he would have a
little bit more flexibility,

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.690
a little less stress, better lifestyle,

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.960
but he's not practicing
the type of medicine

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:15.600
he seems to wanna practice,
and he dreamed of practicing.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.160
So it's always a decision that

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.760
I see my colleagues struggle with

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:20.618
that do they want the intense excitement

00:10:20.618 --> 00:10:23.369
of working in an ER
setting, or do they want

00:10:23.369 --> 00:10:26.620
something more suitable
to their lifestyle,

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.720
that they can start a family,
be attentive to their family,

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:32.280
have a more set schedule
with weekends off.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.860
If you are really passionate
about a specific field

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.830
of medicine, you can't
imagine doing anything else.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.590
So I can totally see Dr
Green saying no to this

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:44.345
and saying I would prefer
to work in the stressful ER

00:10:44.345 --> 00:10:46.749
because that's where his passion lies.

00:10:46.749 --> 00:10:48.649
- Are you afraid to tell me the truth?

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.900
- Your history of coughing blood,

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:58.900
weight loss, and this x-ray,
is suggestive of cancer.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.010
But the diagnoses has not been confirmed,

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:02.836
and it may very well be something else.

00:11:02.836 --> 00:11:06.650
And none of us should jump to
any conclusions until we know.

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:07.650
That's what I think.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:14.630
- How long do I have?

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:25.210
- Six months to a year.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:28.010
- No doctor could look at an x-ray

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:32.910
and from the x-ray just make a guess

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.140
of how long a patient has from
life expectancy standpoint

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:39.750
without a biopsy, bronchoscopy,
without a CAT scan even.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.130
What she was doing in the
first place was correct,

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.470
you have to tell them that
what you suspect it to be,

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:48.010
give your differential, explain
the next step in treatment,

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:50.000
but not to give him a life expectancy.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.010
That's wrong.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:52.420
It's gonna be inaccurate.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:53.440
- You told me how long has it been

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:54.990
since you had your last period?

00:11:56.304 --> 00:11:57.450
- I don't know.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:58.350
- Just think back.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.183
Tell me roughly.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:04.784
- It was after Christmas.

00:12:04.784 --> 00:12:05.980
- Okay.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:07.280
So it's been a few months?

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.720
- I guess so, I haven't
really paid attention.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:14.380
- And you've had sexual intercourse?

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:20.373
- Yes.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:23.543
- Dr Carter?

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:27.090
What you have is an ectopic pregnancy

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.650
and she needs to be scheduled
for ultrasound and surgery.

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:30.483
Right away.

00:12:30.483 --> 00:12:32.580
- What is going on right now?

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:35.020
He asked her when her last period was,

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.950
she said she wasn't sure,
maybe a few months ago.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:41.798
He asked if she had sexual
intercourse, she said yes.

00:12:41.798 --> 00:12:45.987
And he just makes a diagnoses
of an ectopic pregnancy

00:12:45.987 --> 00:12:48.180
without doing a physical exam?

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.960
This is just, this is so inaccurate.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.280
You may suspect an ectopic pregnancy

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.800
from doing a physical exam,
and getting a full history,

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.950
but you don't diagnose someone with that.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:00.240
That's just, it's wild.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.000
She could have so many other things.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.290
She could have, because
it's left lower side

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:05.960
in abdominal pain, it could be

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.795
something with her stool,
she could have a lot of gas,

00:13:08.795 --> 00:13:10.920
she could have diverticulitis,

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.660
obviously not so common in her age group.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.990
Even sometimes appendicitis,
which normally happens

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.700
on the right side, can present with

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:19.710
lower left side in abdominal pain.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.170
She could also have a cyst in her ovary

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:24.490
that could be causing discomfort.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:26.966
So to just say that she
has an ectopic pregnancy

00:13:26.966 --> 00:13:29.630
from that, oh my god that's so wrong.

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.530
- You people standing around for, huh?

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:34.010
- Do the arterial stick.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:35.093
She got a Babinski?

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:36.953
- Yeah.

00:13:36.953 --> 00:13:40.560
- So, a Babinski Reflex is
when you stroke the bottom,

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.550
or the sole of the foot
with a sharp object.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:45.380
Not a very sharp object,
with like a pointy object,

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:47.190
like the back of a reflex hammer.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.960
And if you stroke upwards,
the toes in an adult

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:51.470
are supposed to flex downwards

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.060
as if they're grabbing the object.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:57.140
If they extend away,
and the big toe goes up,

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:01.020
that's actually a sign of
central nervous system damage.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:03.010
And in times of an overdose,

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:05.768
that could be a very bad predictive sign.

00:14:05.768 --> 00:14:08.260
- How did this happen?

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:09.560
- He fell out of his crib.

00:14:11.623 --> 00:14:13.600
- He fell out of his crib.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:17.610
- I mean, he was crying all
night, so I brought him here.

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:19.866
- I bet he's been
crying, when did he fall?

00:14:19.866 --> 00:14:20.925
- I don't know.

00:14:20.925 --> 00:14:22.075
I don't want to get into trouble.

00:14:22.075 --> 00:14:23.424
- Ma'am, your son has
multiple contusions --

00:14:23.424 --> 00:14:24.257
- He's not my son.

00:14:24.257 --> 00:14:25.090
- You know, whatever!

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:27.530
- So, a young child
with multiple bruising,

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.830
multiple contusions, is very suspicious

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.450
for child abuse, and it would need to be

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:34.430
screened by children or family services.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.300
Another thing that makes this suspicious

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:39.540
about child abuse is fractures in

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.000
different stages of healing.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.680
Also a child that is scared to talk

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:43.920
in front of their parents.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.470
Could be an early sign, speak in front

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.250
of their parents, could be an early sign.

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:50.400
Because they're hiding how
they got their injuries

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.150
and lying to you to try and
cover up for their family.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.670
A very unfortunate
common death in children,

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.380
especially young infants,
like six months of age,

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:02.030
three months of age, is
Shaken Baby Syndrome,

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:04.508
where after crying for a
very long period of time,

00:15:04.508 --> 00:15:07.520
frustrated parents will
shake their baby very hard

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.570
to get them to stop and actually cause

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.841
severe spinal and or brain damage,

00:15:11.841 --> 00:15:14.510
which causes the baby to
stop breathing and die.

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.580
It's horrible when this happens.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.260
I've seen this happen.

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:21.840
So that's why I always screen my parents,

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.720
my parents, my patients who are parents,

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:28.280
to find out how they're
doing in raising their child,

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.230
if there's any symptoms of depression,

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:31.810
if they are overly stressed out

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:33.620
because then I try and get them help,

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.776
and give them some stress
management techniques

00:15:35.776 --> 00:15:38.290
and really tell them what to do

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:41.100
in case that they're feeling
these emotions creep in

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.060
so they don't make a
horrible mistake like that.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.260
- He was standing.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.030
Blocking traffic.

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:49.525
But he's got that smell,
so I thought I'd check.

00:15:49.525 --> 00:15:51.440
- You did the right thing.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.790
We've got a diabetic
ketoacidosis, let's go.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:56.090
- Very commonly, folks who are

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:57.624
in diabetic ketoacidosis, which means

00:15:57.624 --> 00:15:59.570
that their blood sugar is so high,

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:02.137
their blood is become acidotic and,

00:16:02.137 --> 00:16:04.920
all the functions, all
the cellular processes

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.640
that go on in your body, start collapsing,

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:10.320
and breaking down, and you can die

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.693
from diabetic ketoacidosis, and what this

00:16:12.693 --> 00:16:15.170
police officer is saying is
you got that smell on him

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.112
is 'cause you have a very fruity aroma

00:16:17.112 --> 00:16:20.070
when you're going through
diabetic ketoacidosis.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:22.230
It's a very distinct smell that
if you've smelled it before

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.080
you know exactly what the
police officer's talking about.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.080
That feeling when you go to sleep,

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.190
and you know you have couple hours

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.040
to nap when you're on call, oh my god

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:40.540
those are the most precious hours

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:42.110
'cause your eyes are just closing.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:44.550
And you're just exhausted,
but it feels so good,

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.720
even like 30 minutes of
sleep, it's really important

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:49.343
to prevent you from going crazy.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:52.040
- [Woman] 6:30, Dr Green.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.384
- And it really does feel like that.

00:16:54.384 --> 00:16:57.760
You slept 10 seconds
and it's been two hours.

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:01.030
ER is a classic, it's
well-written, it's well-produced.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.920
I really could see myself
binge-watching this show.

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.140
I mean, I watched this show when

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:06.531
I was like eight nine years old,

00:17:06.531 --> 00:17:09.780
but now being a doctor,
it's cool watching this

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:12.040
and learning how they did certain things

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:14.800
back in the '90s, what problems they had.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.130
So if you wanna binge this show,

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.660
it's actually available on Hulu right now.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.220
Like I always say, if you
have any recommendations,

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:24.230
or you have some questions,
jump into the comment section.

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:26.730
'Cause you know I respond,
if not down there,

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.010
in my monthly Responding
to Comments video.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.023
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:17:31.893 --> 00:17:34.310
(epic music)

